Subject: AXPpci33 and PCIPCI bridges

Well, it appears that even with the latest firmware, our AXPpci33
systems do _not_ configure PPBs or devices behind PPBs (boo, hiss).  Does
anyone know whether DEC is aware of and/or whether they might fix this?
I'm now looking at having to carry our "evil hack" PPB configuration code into
-current.  Perhaps PCI configuration is done by the SROM code and not the
firmware, I'm not sure.  Does anyone know the latest version for the
AXPpci33 SROM code and have access to it?  I'm very surprised that this issue
hasn't been fixed, as PPBs aren't really obscure or exotic - we're supposed
to be able to have uniquitous multifunction PCI peripherals that contain
them, for example.
-ben

From matt@lkg.dec.com  Thu Jan 9 14:49:33 1997
From: Matt Thomas <matt@lkg.dec.com>
To: tober@bbnplanet.com
Date: Thu, 09 Jan 1997 14:49:33 +0000
Subject: Re: AXPpci33 and PCIPCI bridges

The AXPpci33 is a fairly old design and during it's firmware development
PPBs where just a gleam in some chip designer's eye.  Well, maybe not but
you get the idea.  To add PPB support to the AXPpci33 firmware would be a
significant development effort and due to relatively low volumes of AXPpci33
board sold, it's not cost effective to due so.

So it's highly unlikely that you'll ever PPB support for the AXPpci33.

The EB164 and PC164 motherboards are recent to support PPBs...
